From 470f90c669b7939fa9ad52a579ffc0ad296ab161 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Helmut Januschka <helmut@januschka.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 19:18:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] hires mic: a fixed preamp, so the suppressor can do its
 job

The tracked gain was the wrong instrument. It levelled a voice and the
room to within 2 dB of each other, and the noise suppressor downstream
keys on exactly that difference: fed its output, it stripped the voice
along with the fans and recordings came back at -73 dBFS.

Measured through both sources at once, speech against the room:

  tracked gain, then suppression   -47.6 dBFS   SNR 16.3 dB
  no gain, then suppression        -58.3 dBFS   SNR 21.3 dB
  fixed preamp, then suppression   -29.3 dBFS   SNR 23.0 dB

A suppressor expects roughly the level a microphone gives it, and this
uplink arrives near -51 dBFS, so it is lifted first and the dynamics it
reads are left intact. Peaks measured -14.5 dBFS, which leaves room for
a raised voice.

The test asserts the lift against Preamp::gain() rather than a copy of
the number, after an earlier retune left it asserting the old one.

The call check's third button no longer disables itself once the right
source is selected; it says which one is in use instead, because a
disabled button reads as broken.
